"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][7]={"university_physics_en:M7-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M7-L1 – Reflection, Refraction and Total Internal Reflection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Electromagnetic waves, angle\nmeasurements and trigonometry. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive Snell’s law from phase\ncontinuity and calculate critical angles.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Light at an Interface<\/h2>\n<p>A plane wave arrives from a homogeneous isotropic medium of\nrefractive index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat a second medium\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts frequency is preserved at a stationary interface. In nonmagnetic,\nweakly absorbing materials the phase speed is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}\\approx c/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPropagation angles are measured from the surface normal, not the surface\nitself. Thus the angle of incidence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis between the incident ray and the normal. The reflected ray satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_r=\\theta_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe transmitted ray can change direction because the wavelengths\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_i=\\lambda_0/n_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiffer at fixed frequency. Rays represent energy flow only in the\ngeometrical-optics limit; the full field consists of wave amplitudes\nwhose energy fractions follow from Fresnel theory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Derivation from Phase Matching<\/h2>\n<p>Write the phase of the incident field as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_1=\\boldsymbol{k}_1\\cdot\\boldsymbol{r}-\\omega t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause the spatial and temporal phase must agree at every point on the\ninterface, tangential wave-vector components coincide:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_1\\sin\\theta_1=k_2\\sin\\theta_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i=n_i\\omega/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields Snell’s law immediately:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1\\sin\\theta_1=n_2\\sin\\theta_2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The corresponding condition for the reflected vector yields the law\nof reflection. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1&gt;n_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nno real propagating transmission angle is possible beyond a sufficiently\nlarge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe critical angle obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta_c=n_2/n_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTotal internal reflection then occurs, but an evanescent field exists on\nthe low-index side. A second nearby interface can couple to that field,\nso perfect isolation is not a universal assertion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Air and Glass<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1=1.00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2=1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta_2=(1/1.5)\\sin30^\\circ=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>19.47<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_2\\approx19.47^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the reverse direction, from glass to air, the critical angle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arcsin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>41.81<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_c=\\arcsin(1/1.5)\\approx41.81^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>50<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincidence angle in glass gives total internal reflection. Always draw\nthe normal before indicating ray directions and angles. The diagram also\nshows why a critical angle exists only when passing from larger to\nsmaller refractive index. Low-loss guiding in a fibre requires suitable\ngeometry and modes in addition to the critical-angle condition.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_197_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Rays at an optical interface showing incidence, reflection and refraction\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rays at an optical interface showing\nincidence, reflection and refraction<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Angles are measured from the surface normal.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the refracted angle for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can total internal reflection occur from air\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ninto glass\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)?\nExplain.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the tangential component of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconserved at the interface?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta_2=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>14.48<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_2\\approx14.48^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; a real refracted direction exists for every incident angle\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1&lt;n_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Phase matching along the entire stationary interface requires the\nsame tangential wave number.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditions of Validity<\/h2>\n<p>Snell’s scalar law does not by itself specify reflected intensity,\npolarization or phase jumps. In absorbing materials the refractive index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be complex, and fields and energy flux require more careful\ntreatment. Strongly structured optics or apertures comparable to a\nwavelength exceed ray-optics accuracy. Also check that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis evaluated at the frequency corresponding to the specified vacuum\nwavelength. In a dispersive substance refraction angles depend on\nfrequency, so a white beam can split spectrally.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Reflection and refraction follow from phase continuity. Total\ninternal reflection does not remove the evanescent field. The next\nlesson studies image formation by lenses.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Geometrical Optics: Lenses and\nImaging<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-summary\">University Physics 3 – Chapter 1 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.1, §1.6–1.7; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L1 – Reflection, Refraction and Total Internal Reflection · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-71-optics-spring-2009/pages/lecture-slides/\">2.71 Optics – Lecture Slides<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sitzungen 2–8, 14–19; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L1 – Reflection, Refraction and Total Internal Reflection · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M7-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M7-L2 – Geometrical Optics: Lenses and Imaging<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Refraction, similar triangles and the\nparaxial approximation. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply the thin-lens equation and\ndetermine magnification and image type from signs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Paraxial Ray Model<\/h2>\n<p>In the geometrical-optics limit directed rays describe narrow optical\nbundles. A thin converging lens redirects rays near and parallel to the\noptical axis toward a point on the image side at focal length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA thin diverging lens has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStandard construction rays are: one entering parallel to the axis and\nleaving toward the image-side focus; one entering through the\nobject-side focus and exiting parallel; and one passing approximately\nundeviated through the optical centre. For a real image the actual rays\nmeet. For a virtual image only the backward extensions intersect; it\ncannot be collected directly on a screen.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lens Equation and Signs<\/h2>\n<p>We adopt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a real object before the lens and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a real image behind it. Similar triangles for paraxial rays give<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>f<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mi>b<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1}{f}=\\frac{1}{g}+\\frac{1}{b},\\qquad m=-\\frac{b}{g}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=h&#39;/h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis signed transverse magnification;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans that the image is inverted. Dimensionless magnification should not\nbe confused with a length. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&gt;f&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;g&lt;f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the image is virtual and upright. Imaging is treated to first order\naround the axis. In multiple-lens systems object and image distances\nmust be reconstructed between elements; focal lengths cannot simply be\nadded without conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Object in Front of a Lens<\/h2>\n<p>A converging lens has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0.100\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn object of height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=2.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=0.300\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.100<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.300<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.667<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/b=1/0.100-1/0.300=6.667\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.150\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMagnification is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.150<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.300<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=-0.150/0.300=-0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving image height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h&#39;=-1.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe image is real, reduced and inverted. The drawing marks object\nheight, principal axis, focal points and two construction rays that\nintersect. Move the object between the front focus and the lens in a\nthought experiment: the emerging physical rays no longer intersect.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_292_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Converging lens with an object, image and two construction rays\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Converging lens with an object, image and\ntwo construction rays<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A real image forms where actual rays intersect.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=10\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the image type for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=10\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=5\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does making the aperture arbitrarily small not improve image\nsharpness without limit?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nreal, equal in size and inverted.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/b=1/10-1/5=-1/10\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=-10\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nvirtual and upright.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Diffraction blur increases as the aperture diameter is reduced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Aberrations and Aperture<\/h2>\n<p>The thin-lens equation neglects thickness, large ray angles and\nfrequency-dependent refraction. Real lenses exhibit spherical\naberration, coma, astigmatism and chromatic aberration. The aperture\nlimits rays and changes depth of field as well as diffraction. A smaller\naperture can reduce geometrical aberrations while increasing\ndiffraction; there is an optimum rather than an unconditional\nsmaller-is-better rule. In microscopes and cameras magnification,\ndiffraction-limited resolution and detector sampling must be considered\ntogether.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Sign conventions and validity belong in every lens calculation. The\nfollowing lesson treats reflection amplitudes and polarization.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Polarization and Fresnel\nCoefficients<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/2-summary\">University Physics 3 – Chapter 2 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.1–2.3; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L2 – Geometrical Optics: Lenses and Imaging · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-71-optics-spring-2009/pages/lecture-slides/\">2.71 Optics – Lecture Slides<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sitzungen 2–8, 14–19; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L2 – Geometrical Optics: Lenses and Imaging · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M7-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M7-L3 – Polarization and Fresnel Coefficients<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Electric field as a vector,\ninterfaces and angles. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish s/p polarization,\ncalculate normal-incidence reflectance and apply Malus’s law.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Polarization of Electromagnetic Waves<\/h2>\n<p>For a plane transverse electromagnetic wave, the electric field is\nperpendicular to propagation. Linear polarization means the field\noscillates along a fixed line at a given point. Circular and elliptical\npolarization describe other paths of the field tip over time. The plane\ncontaining the ray and the interface normal is the plane of incidence.\nThe electric field is <strong>s-polarized<\/strong> if perpendicular to\nthat plane, and <strong>p-polarized<\/strong> if in the plane.\nUnpolarized light is commonly modeled as a statistical mixture; it does\nnot have a single deterministic field direction. Reflected and\ntransmitted fractions depend on polarization and incidence angle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Field Amplitudes at an Interface<\/h2>\n<p>Boundary conditions on tangential electric and magnetic fields yield\nthe Fresnel amplitudes. For s-polarization at a lossless, nonmagnetic\ninterface and angles related by Snell’s law, the electric-field\nreflection coefficient is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s=\\frac{n_1\\cos\\theta_1-n_2\\cos\\theta_2}{n_1\\cos\\theta_1+n_2\\cos\\theta_2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For p-polarization, the sign of the amplitude coefficient depends on\nthe respective polarization basis; the unambiguous physical reflectance\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p=|r_p|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt normal incidence both polarizations give<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\left(\\frac{n_1-n_2}{n_1+n_2}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is an <strong>intensity<\/strong> reflectance, not a\nfield-amplitude magnitude. For nonabsorbing interfaces, correctly\nnormalized energy flux obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R+T=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a boundary between nonmagnetic media, p-polarized reflectance\nvanishes at the Brewster angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan\\theta_B=n_2/n_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Glass and Analyser<\/h2>\n<p>For air\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1=1.00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nand glass\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2=1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nnormal incidence gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.50<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2.50<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=(-0.50/2.50)^2=0.040<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.0<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.0\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof incoming intensity at <strong>one<\/strong> uncoated surface. With two\nsurfaces, multiple reflections may need consideration. The Brewster\nangle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arctan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>56.31<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_B=\\arctan(1.5)\\approx56.31^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn ideal linear analyser transmits a fraction<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_0\\cos^2\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>of already linearly polarized light. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>60<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=60^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween the input polarization and transmission axis,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains. The diagram separates the plane of incidence, s/p axes and\nanalyser axis.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_221_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Polarization in the plane of incidence and ideal analyser axes\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Polarization in the plane of incidence\nand ideal analyser axes<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Field direction and intensity must be distinguished.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat a single interface from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an ideal analyser at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|r_s|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot directly be called the fraction of reflected intensity?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11.1<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=((1-2)/(1+2))^2=1/9\\approx11.1\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=\\cos^2(45^\\circ)=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a field-amplitude ratio; intensity reflectance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|r_s|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of Ideal Polarizers<\/h2>\n<p>Malus’s law requires an ideally linearly polarized input and an ideal\nanalyser. For initially unpolarized light, a first ideal polarizer\ntransmits half the intensity on average before the second axis follows\nMalus’s law. Absorbing media, anisotropic crystals or magnetic materials\ngenerally require more than the simple refractive-index formulas. A\nphase delay between s and p components can change polarization without\neliminating the total field. Practical antireflection coatings exploit\ninterference and complex field amplitudes rather than simple geometric\nshading.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Polarization describes fields, not just rays. Diffraction and\ninterference next follow directly from superposition of phase.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Interference, Diffraction and\nOptical Gratings<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-summary\">University Physics 3 – Chapter 1 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.1, §1.6–1.7; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L3 – Polarization and Fresnel Coefficients · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-71-optics-spring-2009/pages/lecture-slides/\">2.71 Optics – Lecture Slides<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sitzungen 2–8, 14–19; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L3 – Polarization and Fresnel Coefficients · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl/tn2421/content/Chap1_Basics/Basics_2022_01Clean.html\">TU Delft – Basic Electromagnetic and Wave Optics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Eq. 139–144, lines 624–638; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L3 – Polarization and Fresnel Coefficients<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M7-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M7-L4 – Interference, Diffraction and Optical Gratings<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Harmonic waves, phase difference and\nsmall angles. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish double- and\nsingle-slit conditions and analyse a grating.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Superposition of Two Waves<\/h2>\n<p>For harmonic fields, optical intensity is proportional to the\ntime-averaged squared field amplitude. When two waves of equal frequency\nand polarization overlap, <strong>fields<\/strong> add before intensities\ndo. For individual intensities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand relative phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_1+I_2+2\\sqrt{I_1I_2}\\cos\\delta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Equal field amplitudes give an ideal zero minimum and a maximum four\ntimes the intensity of a single arm. In a double-slit arrangement with\ncentre spacing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe geometric path difference in far-field direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConstructive interference occurs ideally when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sin\\theta=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℤ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\in\\mathbb Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFringes result from the two sources, while the finite width of either\nslit adds a diffraction envelope.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interference and Diffraction as Phase Calculations<\/h2>\n<p>For a slit of width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncontinuously distributed elementary waves interfere. In the Fraunhofer\nregime the intensity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>β<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(\\theta)=I(0)\\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2,\\qquad \\beta=\\frac{\\pi a\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>using the continuous limit at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDark minima occur at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\sin\\theta=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA grating of many equally spaced slits has narrow principal maxima at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sin\\theta=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthat condition determines the <em>position<\/em> of principal orders, not\nautomatically their strengths. With finite slit widths the grating peaks\nare modulated by the single-slit envelope. Fluctuating relative phase\nwashes out the interference term in a long exposure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Fringe Spacing<\/h2>\n<p>For two narrow slits, a screen at distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\approx L\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat small angles. Fringe spacing is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x\\approx\\lambda L/d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=2.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.50\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=2.4\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA grating with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=2.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas its first principal order for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arcsin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>17.46<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1=\\arcsin(0.30)\\approx17.46^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram contrasts double-slit fringes with a single-slit envelope\nand marks angles from the optical axis. At large angles use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirectly rather than replacing it by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_136_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Double slit with fringe intensity curve under a single-slit envelope\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Double slit with fringe intensity curve\nunder a single-slit envelope<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Double-slit fringes lie inside a diffraction envelope.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.25\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the first single-slit minimum for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are grating maxima not infinitely narrow, arbitrarily high\nlines?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=2.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>2.87<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\approx2.87^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Finite slit number and width produce finite angular widths and\nconstrain peak intensity.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Coherence and Finite Apertures<\/h2>\n<p>Constructive interference requires a sufficiently well-defined phase\nrelationship. Sunlight can interfere after spatial and spectral\nfiltering, but two independent lamps cannot automatically be treated\nlike a coherent two-arm source. Far-field formulas assume a detection\ngeometry in which path differences can be expressed by an angular\ncoordinate. A finite grating has peaks of finite width and secondary\nmaxima. Polarization, detector sampling, stray light and slit\nimperfections also influence the observed pattern. Measured fringe\npositions must be analysed with both geometry and wavelength in\nmind.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Superposition yields double-slit fringes and their diffraction\nenvelopes. Fourier optics generalizes this to arbitrary apertures.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Fourier Optics and Resolving\nPower<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/3-summary\">University Physics 3 – Chapter 3 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §3.1–3.2; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L4 – Interference, Diffraction and Optical Gratings · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-summary\">University Physics 3 – Chapter 4 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.2–4.5; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L4 – Interference, Diffraction and Optical Gratings · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-71-optics-spring-2009/pages/lecture-slides/\">2.71 Optics – Lecture Slides<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sitzungen 2–8, 14–19; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L4 – Interference, Diffraction and Optical Gratings · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M7-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M7-L5 – Fourier Optics and Resolving Power<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Complex exponentials, integration and\ndiffraction. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret the Fraunhofer field\nas a Fourier transform of the pupil and calculate diffraction\nlimits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Pupil as a Complex Field<\/h2>\n<p>Every finite aperture truncates the incident electromagnetic field. A\ncomplex pupil function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes this action: its magnitude specifies transmitted field\namplitude and its argument the phase shift. Two openings of equal\ngeometrical area can produce different diffraction patterns if their\namplitude and phase profiles differ. Coherent imaging propagates field\namplitude; incoherent illumination requires averaging intensities under\nsuitable assumptions. A lens can make the spatial-frequency content\nvisible in its focal plane. Fourier optics connects this diffraction\nprocess to a mathematical transform of the complex optical field.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Spatial Field to Far Field<\/h2>\n<p>In scalar Fraunhofer theory, the far-field amplitude at angular\ncoordinates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_x,\\theta_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis proportional to<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mo>∬<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(\\theta_x,\\theta_y)\\propto\\iint P(x,y)\\exp\\!\\left[-i\\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}(x\\sin\\theta_x+y\\sin\\theta_y)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dx\\,\\mathrm dy.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The detector measures\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\propto|U|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso intensity alone does not preserve all Fourier phase information. A\ncomputation must specify its Fourier-transform convention (sign and\nnormalization). For a circular, uniformly illuminated aperture, an\nAiry-type diffraction pattern follows. Its first dark angular ring\noccurs approximately at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.22<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\approx1.22\\lambda/D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor small angles, where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis aperture diameter. The Rayleigh criterion considers two point images\njust resolved when separated by this angle; other criteria produce\ndifferent thresholds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Circular Aperture<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal telescope with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>550<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=550\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas Rayleigh angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.22<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>550<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.71<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_R\\approx1.22\\cdot550\\times10^{-9}/0.10=6.71\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.38<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.38<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narcseconds. Doubling the diameter halves this diffraction-angle limit at\nfixed wavelength. The diagram shows a circular pupil and its broad\ncentral diffraction maximum surrounded by weaker rings. In laboratories\nreal point-spread functions differ because of defocus, aberrations and\nthe measured optical bandwidth. The calculated Rayleigh limit is an\nideal diffraction-geometric measure, not a guarantee of separation for\nevery possible object.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_105_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Circular aperture with a schematic radial diffraction-intensity plot\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Circular aperture with a schematic radial\ndiffraction-intensity plot<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The central peak is not a point.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis tripled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is measured far-field intensity sometimes insufficient to\nreconstruct the pupil?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It decreases to one third.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.05<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_R=3.05\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Intensity gives the squared modulus of the Fourier amplitude, but\nnot its phase.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Resolution Is a Criterion<\/h2>\n<p>The Rayleigh criterion is not an absolute information bound: noise,\ncontrast, pixel sampling, prior information and reconstruction\nalgorithms influence what can be distinguished. A larger image is not\nnecessarily better resolved; magnification and the physical point-spread\nfunction are distinct quantities. Scalar theory fails for strongly\nfocused, high-numerical-aperture vector fields. To compare real\ninstruments, examine the measured point-spread function, modulation\ntransfer and illumination conditions rather than just one formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The pupil determines the far field through a Fourier relation.\nTemporal pulses also have spectra; their propagation leads to\ndispersion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Dispersion, Group Delay and\nOptical Pulses<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-summary\">University Physics 3 – Chapter 4 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.2–4.5; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L5 – Fourier Optics and Resolving Power · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-71-optics-spring-2009/pages/lecture-slides/\">2.71 Optics – Lecture Slides<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sitzungen 2–8, 14–19; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L5 – Fourier Optics and Resolving Power · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M7-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M7-L6 – Dispersion, Group Delay and Optical Pulses<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Wave packets, derivatives and\nfrequency spectra. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish phase and group\nvelocities and compute group delay from the group index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Monochromatic Field to Pulse<\/h2>\n<p>A real optical pulse does not contain just one frequency. It is a\nsuperposition of spectral components with different frequencies and,\npotentially, different wavenumbers. The phase of one harmonic component\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp[i(kz-\\omega t)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntravels at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}=\\omega/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy contrast, the envelope of a narrow-band packet follows group motion.\nWhen material response depends on frequency, these two velocities change\ndifferently. “Speed of light in a material” is therefore ambiguous\nwithout qualification: phase, group, energy and information speeds must\nbe distinguished. The vacuum speed of light\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains the fundamental limit for causal signaling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Wavenumber and Group Velocity<\/h2>\n<p>For an isotropic medium with frequency-dependent phase index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n(\\omega)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(\\omega)=n(\\omega)\\omega/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLinearizing near carrier frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(\\omega)\\approx k_0+k_1(\\omega-\\omega_0),\\qquad k_1=\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dk}{\\mathrm d\\omega}\\right|_{\\omega_0}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The envelope is approximately delayed by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=k_1L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>c<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_g=\\frac{1}{k_1}=\\frac{c}{n_g},\\qquad n_g=n+\\omega\\frac{\\mathrm dn}{\\mathrm d\\omega}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_2=\\mathrm d^2k/\\mathrm d\\omega^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nspecifies group-velocity dispersion. A finite-bandwidth pulse can\nbroaden, or initially compress if suitably prechirped. The Fourier\ntime-bandwidth relationship depends on precisely defined pulse shapes\nand width conventions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Fibre Delay<\/h2>\n<p>An optical guide of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1.00\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas group index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.47<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g=1.47<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the carrier frequency. Group delay is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.47<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.99792458<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_g=n_gL/c\\approx1.47\\times1000/(2.99792458\\times10^8)\\,\\mathrm s\\approx4.90\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe same distance in vacuum takes about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.34<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.34\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis compares pulse group delay in a transparent, weakly distorted band;\nit does not describe the arrival of a new causal disturbance’s leading\nedge. The figure compares a narrow input profile with an output\nbroadened by different group delays of spectral components. A measurable\ngroup delay does not guarantee unchanged pulse shape.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_198_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Narrow input pulse and broadened output pulse\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Narrow input pulse and broadened output\npulse<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The envelope changes width during propagation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=100\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which index sets the group delay,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is “a short pulse is monochromatic” false?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_g=1.5\\cdot100/c\\approx0.500\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g=n+\\omega\\,\\mathrm dn/\\mathrm d\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Short pulses require a broad frequency spectrum by the Fourier\nrelation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pulse Spreading and Signal Transport<\/h2>\n<p>The elementary relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_g=(\\mathrm dk/\\mathrm d\\omega)^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis especially useful for narrowband, weakly absorbed pulses. In strongly\nabsorbing or resonant regions, unusual and even negative group delays\nmay occur without information traveling backward in time. In long\nfibres, modal dispersion, polarization and nonlinear effects also\nmatter. A shorter temporal pulse has a broader spectrum and may\ntherefore experience more frequency-dependent group delay. The sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone cannot predict the evolution of every pulse without its initial\nshape and phase profile.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Phase and group delays differ. The next lesson asks when two optical\nfields can interfere with stable contrast.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Optical Coherence and\nInterferometry<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-71-optics-spring-2009/pages/calendar/\">2.71 Optics – Calendar<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sitzungen 13, 15, 22; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L6 – Dispersion, Group Delay and Optical Pulses · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-977-ultrafast-optics-spring-2005/resources/chapter2/\">6.977 Ultrafast Optics – chapter2.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ressourcenbeschreibung, lines 118–132; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L6 – Dispersion, Group Delay and Optical Pulses · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-03sc-physics-iii-vibrations-and-waves-fall-2016/pages/part-ii-electromagnetic-waves/lecture-13/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – 8.03SC Lecture 13: Dispersive Medium, Phase Velocity, Group Velocity (2016)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lecture title; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L6 – Dispersion, Group Delay and Optical Pulses · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M7-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M7-L7 – Optical Coherence and Interferometry<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Interference, complex fields and time\naveraging. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret coherence and\nvisibility and calculate path differences in a Michelson\ninterferometer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Temporal and Spatial Coherence<\/h2>\n<p>Interference requires relative phase to remain sufficiently stable\nduring measurement. <strong>Temporal coherence<\/strong> characterizes\nthe correlation of a field with a delayed copy of itself.\n<strong>Spatial coherence<\/strong> describes correlations at different\ntransverse points. Both concepts refer to statistical field states and\nto measurement arrangements, not absolute labels for “coherent” and\n“incoherent” light. A laser may have high coherence, but real lasers\nhave finite spectral width and phase noise. With broadband light,\ncontrast generally decreases once the arm delay exceeds the coherence\ntime. Spatial and spectral filtering can increase contrast, usually at\nthe expense of optical power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Visibility and Correlation Function<\/h2>\n<p>For a stationary scalar field define the normalized first-order\ncoherence function<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g^{(1)}(\\tau)=\\frac{\\langle E^*(t)E(t+\\tau)\\rangle}{\\langle|E(t)|^2\\rangle}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For two arms of intensities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe averaged interference intensity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_1+I_2+2\\sqrt{I_1I_2}\\,\\mathrm{Re}\\!\\left[g^{(1)}(\\tau)e^{i\\phi}\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Visibility\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=(I_{\\max}-I_{\\min})/(I_{\\max}+I_{\\min})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=2\\sqrt{I_1I_2}|g^{(1)}(\\tau)|/(I_1+I_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this ideal model. With balanced arms,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=|g^{(1)}(\\tau)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA characteristic coherence length is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∼<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_c\\sim c\\tau_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin vacuum; the exact numerical factor depends on spectral shape and how\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis defined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Michelson Mirror<\/h2>\n<p>In a Michelson interferometer a beamsplitter creates two arms. The\ntwo beams return after reflection and interfere at the output. Moving\none mirror by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges the optical path difference in air by approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\ell=2\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor vacuum wavelength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>632.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=632.8\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na displacement of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>316.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=\\lambda/2=316.4\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives a complete\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphase cycle, corresponding to <strong>one<\/strong> passing interference\nperiod. If ten bright fringes pass a fixed detector, the mirror\ndisplacement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.164<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\lambda/2=3.164\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the ideal model. The figure emphasizes the doubled path change, a\ncommon source of mistakes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_175_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Michelson setup with a beamsplitter and two arms\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Michelson setup with a beamsplitter and\ntwo arms<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Mirror displacement changes the round-trip path by twice as\nmuch.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How far does a Michelson mirror move for five fringes at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor equal arm intensities and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g^{(1)}|=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can unequal arm intensities reduce contrast even at perfect\ncoherence?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=5\\lambda/2=1.25\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The interference term scales as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt{I_1I_2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile the background scales as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1+I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nequality optimizes the ratio.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement Limits and Spectra<\/h2>\n<p>Visibility is a direct measure of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g^{(1)}|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonly when arm intensities and polarization states are controlled.\nVibration, drift, detector bandwidth and spatial overlap affect observed\ncontrast. A coherence time cannot be universally equated to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout specifying spectral shape and definitions. A multi-peaked\nspectrum may cause an oscillating coherence function that a single\nthreshold distance describes poorly. Precision measurements must\nadditionally calibrate the refractive index of air, thermal expansion,\noptical alignment and the sign of mirror movement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Coherence links spectra to observed visibility. The last optics\nlesson treats gain and feedback in lasers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Lasers: Stimulated Emission and\nCavity Threshold<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-71-optics-spring-2009/pages/calendar/\">2.71 Optics – Calendar<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sitzungen 13, 15, 22; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L7 – Optical Coherence and Interferometry · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-717j-optical-engineering-spring-2002/pages/lecture-notes/\">2.717J Optical Engineering – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 11–18; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L7 – Optical Coherence and Interferometry · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M7-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M7-L8 – Lasers: Stimulated Emission and Cavity Threshold<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Photon energy, transitions, optical\nintensity and logarithms. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain population inversion and\ncorrectly calculate the gain threshold of a laser.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gain and Pumping<\/h2>\n<p>A laser relies on stimulated emission in a suitable gain medium. An\napplied electromagnetic field can stimulate an excited transition to\nemit another photon in the same optical mode. Pumping creates the\nnonequilibrium populations from which optical amplification can arise.\nFor the relevant transition an effective <strong>population\ninversion<\/strong> is needed: stimulated-emission gain must exceed\nabsorption. A cavity repeatedly feeds a fraction of the light back\nthrough the medium, while a partially transmitting mirror extracts\nuseful output. LASER stands for light amplification by stimulated\nemission of radiation. Not every real laser is perfectly monochromatic,\nperfectly coherent or restricted to one spatial or temporal mode.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Round-Trip Gain and Loss<\/h2>\n<p>For a linear cavity of physical length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the <strong>intensity reflectances<\/strong> of the two mirrors.\nDefine small-signal gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand internal loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas exponential intensity coefficients in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDuring one round trip through the medium the light traverses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmultiplying intensity by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_{\\mathrm{rt}}=R_1R_2\\exp\\!\\left[2(g-\\alpha)L\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At threshold gain exactly balances loss:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_{\\mathrm{rt}}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsequently<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\mathrm{th}}=\\alpha+\\frac{1}{2L}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1R_2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is <strong>not<\/strong> an electric-field amplitude formula; the\nfactors change if one uses field gain or different loss conventions.\nThreshold means cancellation of linear small-signal losses, not\nunlimited exponential output-power growth.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Threshold Gain<\/h2>\n<p>Consider an ideal cavity with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.300\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.990<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=0.990<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.900<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2=0.900<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.0200\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.891<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1R_2=0.891<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0200<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.891<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>0.600<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.212<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\mathrm{th}}=0.0200+\\frac{\\ln(1/0.891)}{0.600}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}\\approx0.212\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The required small-signal gain exceeds internal loss. Below threshold\nthe round-trip multiplier is less than unity; above threshold the field\nmay grow from noise until gain saturation and other nonlinear mechanisms\nlimit it to a steady state. For a uniform cavity of index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe approximate longitudinal mode spacing is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu\\approx c/(2nL)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram distinguishes round-trip path, mirror loss and exponential\ngain.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_147_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Laser cavity with two mirrors and a gain medium\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Laser cavity with two mirrors and a gain\nmedium<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Gain and mirror losses are balanced per round trip.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\mathrm{th}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=R_2=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.1\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which mirror quantity enters the stated threshold: amplitude\nreflectivity or intensity reflectance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.50\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\mathrm{th}}=\\alpha=0.1\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The stated\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare intensity reflectances.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=c/(2L)\\approx300\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lasers Beyond the Ideal Model<\/h2>\n<p>The threshold expression assumes constant exponential coefficients\nbased on the same intensity convention and a linear cavity. Distributed\ngain, modal profiles, scattering loss, facet effects, polarization\ncoupling and spatial hole burning require more detailed models for\ncomplex lasers. Threshold alone says nothing about efficiency, beam\nquality, eye safety, linewidth or pulse duration. High optical power can\npermanently damage biological tissue; alignment and measurement\ntherefore require appropriate laser-safety procedures and are not given\nas hands-on instructions here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Lasers combine stimulated emission, optical feedback and a defined\nloss balance. Quantum and solid-state courses later extend these optical\nfoundations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/8-summary\">University Physics 3 – Chapter 8 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §8.6; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L8 – Lasers: Stimulated Emission and Cavity Threshold · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/8-6-lasers\">University Physics 3 – Lasers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §8.6, lines 30–33; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L8 – Lasers: Stimulated Emission and Cavity Threshold<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Electricity_and_Magnetism/Electromagnetics_and_Applications_(Staelin)/12:_Optical_Communications/12.03:_Lasers\">LibreTexts – D. H. Staelin, Electromagnetics and Applications, 12.3 Lasers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Eq. 12.3.7, lines 254–258; Subject relevance to lesson: M7-L8 – Lasers: Stimulated Emission and Cavity Threshold<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
